 Suppose that the order of selection does not matter and no student can serve on more than one committee. To solve this, I can use the combination formula to find the number of ways to choose 4 students out of 6 without considering the order. The combination formula is \( C(n, k) = \frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \), where \( n \) is the total number of items, \( k \) is the number of chosen items. Substituting the given numbers, we have \( C(6, 4) = \frac{6!}{4!2!} = \frac{720}{24 \times 2} = 15 \). Therefore, there are 15 different ways to form such a committee.
